  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a thorough explanation:\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\n### Do Only Men Have One?\n\nAdam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n### Why Is It Larger in Men?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Hormonal differences:** The Adam's apple is about the same size in males and females until puberty. Once puberty starts, hormones \u2014 especially testosterone \u2014 in males may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n2. **Greater laryngeal growth:** Girls also undergo changes to their voice box during puberty, but the degree of laryngeal growth in females isn't as significant as in males, so most women don't have a prominent Adam's apple.\n\n3. **Shape differences:** There is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n### Why Does a Larger Voice Box Mean a Deeper Voice?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one.\n\n### Can Women Have a More Prominent Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple. Conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can cause an increase in testosterone levels in women, potentially contributing to a more prominent laryngeal prominence.\n\nIn summary, men have larger Adam's apples primarily because of the surge in testosterone during puberty, which causes the larynx to grow significantly more in males than in females, resulting in a more pronounced bump in the throat.",
